WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.260
- So, what are we doing here today?

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.030
- We're gonna do a waste characterization

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.850
of all of Buzzfeed's trash for a day

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.100
for the purpose of figuring out

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.060
if there's an opportunities
to reduce waste,

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.080
maybe by adding a recycling program,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.907
or expanding on your composting program.

00:00:11.907 --> 00:00:14.407
- So, my goal is that Buzzfeed

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.040
reduces our total waste by 20%.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:17.873
Do you think that's realistic?

00:00:17.873 --> 00:00:19.300
- We're gonna have to wait and see.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.550
Once we start digging through the trash

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.087
and see what the opportunities are.

00:00:22.087 --> 00:00:24.920
(energetic music)

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:32.420
- Thank you guys all for volunteering.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.950
Jonathan is our eco expert here.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.840
And he's gonna be telling
us what we're doing.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:37.680
- So, we're literally gonna be

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.230
opening up the bags of
trash, dumping it out,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.923
and trying to separate
it, the material by type.

00:00:42.957 --> 00:00:44.187
- What are the different categories

00:00:44.187 --> 00:00:45.300
we're going to be sorting into?

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.390
- [Jonathan] We're just gonna
do some broad categories.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:49.490
Cardboard, mixed paper,
which would be like,

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.010
office paper, that sort of thing.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.653
Food waste, food soiled paper.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.690
So, maybe like napkins and wrappers.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.240
And then, just kind of a general category

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.827
for other recyclables that
we don't need to specify.

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:01.923
- Anything else?

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:03.520
- [Jonathan] Let's have some fun.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:04.907
- We're just gonna start
digging in trash now?

00:01:04.907 --> 00:01:06.795
- Absolutely.
(volunteers cheer)

00:01:06.795 --> 00:01:08.845
- Hey, hey, hey, hey, hey!

00:01:08.845 --> 00:01:13.250
(trash clangs)
- Oh.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.410
Any idea what we do with these chip bags?

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:16.610
- Trash.
- Yeah, trash.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.970
'Cause really, they're multilayer.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.540
They're like metal on the inside,

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:20.970
kind of a plastic film on the outside.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.840
So, these would actually
be trash, as well.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:24.783
And we call it solid waste.

00:01:24.805 --> 00:01:26.000
- So, what about this cup?

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.600
- Unless it says on it that
it's certified compostable,

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.080
all these coffee cups have a
plastic liner on the inside,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.923
so this is actually trash.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.370
- Hey, you guys, hey, you guys.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:39.493
This goes in the recycling,
glass is 100% recyclable!

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.940
- [Woman] Oh, what if we find batteries?

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:42.873
- [Jonathan] Put them aside.

00:01:42.873 --> 00:01:44.370
Like, that should go in electric waste.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.640
- I found a whole thing of unopened meat

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:48.397
that says, "Drinks soon?"

00:01:49.504 --> 00:01:52.014
So, paper towels is food soiled?

00:01:52.014 --> 00:01:53.490
- Food soiled paper, unless
it came out of the bathroom,

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.400
then we'll keep it separate.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.450
Hey, guys, we got our first bag to weigh.

00:01:56.455 --> 00:01:58.833
We got a seven pound bag of trash.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.620
We only have 193 pounds to go.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.750
The reason why we're separating

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.580
the food from the food soiled paper

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.790
is because for the purpose of you guys

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.480
expanding your composting
program that you have here,

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.120
you wouldn't wanna put
any of the paper in it.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.560
You'd wanna put only the food waste.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.820
- So, I took out of the bag a completely

00:02:14.893 --> 00:02:17.283
unused pile of paper napkins.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.563
Oh, my God.
- There's more.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.683
- Like, we can actually reuse these.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.286
Right, or no?
(all laugh)

00:02:24.286 --> 00:02:27.040
- Oh, my God, you guys throw away

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.100
so many aluminum cans in the
trash, and glass bottles.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.870
Do you hear that?
(trash clangs)

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.223
- We found some Pero Like
members in the trash, Jonathan.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.230
- [Jonathan] What's it made out of?

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:37.870
- [Woman] It's like a foam core.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:38.703
- [Jonathan] Trash.

00:02:38.703 --> 00:02:40.633
- They unfortunately
go right in the trash.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:42.033
Sorry, Pero Like.

00:02:42.853 --> 00:02:44.930
- [Jonathan] Oh, it's
a heavy one, 20 pounds.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:47.503
This one is 9.6 pounds.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.420
- We got a new amazing volunteer,

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:50.930
'cause we had so much trash.
- Yeah.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:52.800
- So, Ryan's coming to help out.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.350
- So, when I was opening up this bag,

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:57.510
I found inside it, many
bags of just bagels

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.050
that have looked like they are unused.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:02.643
So, one bag, two bags, three bags.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:06.573
Let's see what else, oh, look
it, another bag of bagels.

00:03:06.611 --> 00:03:09.803
- Oh, my God, this is all
the food waste so far.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:10.643
- [Woman] Wow.

00:03:10.643 --> 00:03:12.640
- I'm learning that we
could do a lot better.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.760
I feel like people don't think

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.410
about what they're throwing
away, they just toss it.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.283
- Come on out, thanks for coming.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:19.660
Do you want a hug?
- No.

00:03:20.083 --> 00:03:22.250
- It smells great, right?
- Mm-hmm.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.270
- So, all of this trash is just half

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.030
of the trash that
Buzzfeed makes in one day.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.659
- Oh, wow.
- Just 50%.

00:03:27.659 --> 00:03:30.492
(energetic music)

00:03:35.941 --> 00:03:37.301
- [Jonathan] Yeah!

00:03:37.301 --> 00:03:39.884
(all whooping)

00:03:40.869 --> 00:03:42.619
- Good job, everyone.

00:03:43.531 --> 00:03:44.831
- Gloves are off (laughs).

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.341
- Air high five.
- Thank you!

00:03:47.341 --> 00:03:48.840
Thank you, everyone!

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:49.810
- Thank you, everybody.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:51.240
All right, are you ready for the results

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.411
of the waste characterization?

00:03:52.411 --> 00:03:53.460
- Yes.
- You wanna guess

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:57.603
what percentage of the
trash was actually trash?

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.730
- 50%?
- 36%.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:03.830
So, about one third of what's in

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:05.580
the trash bin is actually trash.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.533
The other two thirds are
recycling or compost.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:09.720
- Yikes.
- I counted

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.993
about 100 pounds worth of recycling.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.670
140 pounds of compost.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.603
And about 135 pounds of trash.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:20.670
- So, my goal of reducing
all of Buzzfeed LA's

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.890
trash by 20%, do you
think that's possible?

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:23.850
- It's possible.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.150
It might be hard to
gather all the recycling.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:27.970
But if you were to recycle all the bottles

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.460
and cans that are being thrown away.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.120
And then, add or expand
on the compost program

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:33.700
to get all that food
waste out of the trash,

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.930
you could definitely beat the 20% goal.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:39.330
- So, I'm gonna take
you over to facilities

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.233
to have you present this information.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.890
And hopefully we can get
all on the same page,

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.590
and try to reduce some of
the trash we make here.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:48.423
- Sounds great.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.140
- Okay, thank you for meeting with us.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:54.393
Basically, we did a
trash sort this morning.

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.390
And Jonathan is gonna tell us the results.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.030
- So, about two thirds of
what was in the trash bin

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.263
is actually recyclable or compostable.

00:05:02.263 --> 00:05:03.553
- Two thirds of it?

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:04.413
- [Jonathan] Yeah, about two thirds.

00:05:04.413 --> 00:05:05.550
- Oh, what a shame.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.890
- But it's a great opportunity
to target some of the items.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.840
The big hitters are a
lot of bottles and cans.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:11.940
- Absolutely.
- What can we do?

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.493
- Interesting, yeah.
- Education.

00:05:14.573 --> 00:05:16.250
The first step is putting

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.440
a recycle bin with every trash can.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:20.960
And then, also labeling
specifically what items

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.220
that are common at Buzzfeed
that can go in there.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:24.430
- I haven't given up on my goal

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:26.400
to reduce our waste by 20%.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.840
And I'm gonna be working with facilities

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:28.960
more to make this happen.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.110
- I think it really emphasized to me,

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:33.160
like how much I should be
reducing, and not just recycling.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.910
- It made me realize that
I need to be more conscious

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.300
as to where I put, you know, my waste.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.370
Especially, the cans.

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:41.330
- [Woman] Compared to other
waste characterizations

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.283
and companies you've
done, how bad is Buzzfeed?

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:45.800
- [Jonathan] You guys have recycle bins.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.520
So, you're doing better
than a lot of businesses

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.470
that still don't have recycling at all.

